@meta
id: 03-12
title: Carry on where you stopped
chapter: 03
type: lesson
skills: insert-I, open-o
par: 64
minutes: 5
vscode: scroll down, scroll back, find your place again
open: order.entity.ts

@brief
`Order` needs a channel field, directly under `customerId`. The type it
should be annotated with is declared at the bottom of this file, and the
brief is not going to tell you its name — go and read it.

So the job has a shape you will meet constantly: start writing a line,
realise you need to check something elsewhere, go and look, come back and
finish the line. The coming back is the part worth learning. Vim
remembers the exact spot where you last stopped typing, down to the
column, and will drop you back into insert mode there.

Work it in that order, even though you could cheat by reading the file
first. The keystroke count assumes the round trip.

@keys
| `o` | open a line below |
| `j` | down a line |
| `gi` | insert again, exactly where insert mode last stopped |
| `Esc` | out |

@hints
Getting back to a line with `k` costs one press per line, and you have
twenty-odd lines to climb. There is a two-key way that does not care how
far away you went.
--
`gi` — `g` then `i` — puts you back in insert mode at the position where
you last left it. Not the start of that line: the exact column.
--
`jj` `o`, type `channel: ` and stop, `Esc`. Read the bottom of the file
with `j`. Then `gi`, type the type name and the semicolon, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    j j o channel:<Space> <Esc>   j×22   gi OrderChannel; <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Fifty-five keystrokes, and only two of them were spent getting back.

`gi` works off a mark that vim updates every time you leave insert mode,
called `^`. Chapter 17 is about marks and how to set your own; this one is
free and automatic, and it is the one you will use most. Two places it
earns its keep immediately:

- You stop typing to look something up, anywhere in the file. `gi` and you
  are back mid-word, not hunting for the line.
- You `Esc` and `:w` out of habit, then want to keep writing. `gi`
  continues the sentence. `A` would only get you to the end of the line,
  which is not the same place if you were typing in the middle of it.

There is a related pair worth knowing the names of now, even though they
belong to chapter 17: `` `. `` jumps to the position of the last change,
and `` `` `` jumps back to where you were before the last big jump. `gi`
is the insert-mode member of that family.

The habit this replaces is the bad one from the last lesson: staying in
insert mode *because leaving felt expensive*. It is not expensive. Leaving
and coming back costs you `Esc` and two keys, and in between you get the
whole editor.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

export type OrderLine = {
  id: string;
  orderId: string;
  sku: string;
  quantity: number;
  unitPrice: number;
};

export type OrderSummary = {
  order: Order;
  lines: OrderLine[];
  refundedTotal: number;
};

export type OrderChannel = 'WEB' | 'IOS' | 'ANDROID' | 'PHONE';

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  channel: OrderChannel;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

export type OrderLine = {
  id: string;
  orderId: string;
  sku: string;
  quantity: number;
  unitPrice: number;
};

export type OrderSummary = {
  order: Order;
  lines: OrderLine[];
  refundedTotal: number;
};

export type OrderChannel = 'WEB' | 'IOS' | 'ANDROID' | 'PHONE';
